Thank you.

22
00:01:46,640 --> 00:01:54,320
For me, quite simply, I love opportunities where you have a business model that fundamentally disrupts an industry.

23
00:01:54,800 --> 00:02:00,800
I'll set it another way, you and your business and products have a bigger margin than your competitors.

24
00:02:01,360 --> 00:02:05,760
And so that's kind of been the thesis behind a lot of my career and what I've worked on.

25
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:16,080
And so we look at apps, what took Coca Cola, what, 60 years and 20 billion dollars to build, any app publisher gets it for free from day one.

26
00:02:16,400 --> 00:02:18,880
And that's global scale and distribution, right?

27
00:02:19,120 --> 00:02:24,400
Every single app sells every single second of every day in every country around the world for free.

28
00:02:24,720 --> 00:02:31,040
And if you think about the growth of commerce and business, Coca Cola took decades to build this.

29
00:02:31,600 --> 00:02:32,720
Nike, decades.

30
00:02:33,360 --> 00:02:34,800
And we get it day one for free.

31
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:36,240
That's unparalleled.

32
00:02:37,040 --> 00:02:40,560
So I love the ability to harness an advantage like that.

33
00:02:40,960 --> 00:02:48,080
And then secondly, when we talk about apps, there are two other key things in my thesis about why I love the app industry.

34
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:52,000
One is the app store rankings basically become a mote for you.

35
00:02:52,400 --> 00:03:04,480
If you can get to the top of a category or even the top of a niche section of a category, it becomes self-fulfilling because you come up highest on search, you get the most users, you get the highest ranking and then the cycle repeats.

36
00:03:04,880 --> 00:03:10,960
The last piece is there's a whole world within the app industry where I say apps complete the app.

37
00:03:13,440 --> 00:03:16,400
I'll give you an example of an app that we bought, which is iNavex.

38
00:03:16,880 --> 00:03:18,240
We're a marine navigation app.

39
00:03:18,560 --> 00:03:20,400
Think of us as Google Maps for the water.

40
00:03:20,880 --> 00:03:24,720
We all know the parallel use case, which is your car's GPS.

41
00:03:25,120 --> 00:03:32,400
You're going to go down the road this weekend and go buy a Toyota Camry, you're going to be paying $2,000 to $2,500 for the built navigation.

42
00:03:32,720 --> 00:03:36,560
And when you drive the car home that afternoon, you're going to take out your phone and use Google Maps for free.

43
00:03:37,120 --> 00:03:46,240
I love when a mobile app can replace a tangible product because again, in that competitive landscape, we're going to win.

44
00:03:46,640 --> 00:03:55,520
Is the average person going to get in the car the car, drive down to the store, look at the shelves, take something to the cash register, swipe a credit card, come home before they even try the product?

45
00:03:56,240 --> 00:03:58,240
Or are they going to sit on their couch and download an app?

46
00:03:58,720 --> 00:04:02,400
And there are products out there where a mobile app will replace a tangible product.

47
00:04:02,960 --> 00:04:05,760
And so, you know, marine navigation is a great example of that.

48
00:04:06,160 --> 00:04:19,520
And so you put all three of those together, and I just think we've had an opportunity, the past decade and obviously the decades coming, to fundamentally change or better compete in kind of global commerce with mobile apps.

49
00:04:19,920 --> 00:04:26,640
I want to interject and call back to an episode we had with Eric Stromberg, I think last year, David, where he posed that the app store.

50
00:04:26,640 --> 00:04:30,240
The App Store is probably the biggest marketplace in human history.

51
00:04:30,560 --> 00:04:36,000
Like there's probably never been a single larger uniform marketplace ever constructed.

52
00:04:36,240 --> 00:04:38,720
And that alone should give you chills, right?

53
00:04:38,880 --> 00:04:40,560
And I think that's exactly what you're hitting on there.

54
00:04:40,720 --> 00:04:43,840
It's like for free and someone's going to chafe and go, Oh, it's thirty percent.

55
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:45,360
But no, no, no, listen.

56
00:04:45,840 --> 00:04:53,360
Like I think the analogy of these consumer companies from the 20th century is really apt because that was the very dawn of globalization, right?

57
00:04:53,600 --> 00:04:55,520
Like you kind of had to build the roads yourself.

58
00:04:55,600 --> 00:05:04,160
And then we come in in in the 21st century and kind of all the roads have been built for us and we just get to distribute and deliver product, which is fascinating and fantastic.

59
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:04,480
So.

60
00:05:05,040 --> 00:05:16,000
Yeah, and actually I'd love to add a 3.5 to your list and that's how apps augment physical activities and other parts of life.

61
00:05:16,320 --> 00:05:19,520
So actually, and we're going to have one of the co founders on the podcast.

62
00:05:19,760 --> 00:05:25,120
I think the next episode is an app called Fitbod and I'm using it to lift weights in my garage now.

63
00:05:25,760 --> 00:05:29,600
And for I don't even know what I'm paying because I don't care because it's so valuable to me.

64
00:05:29,760 --> 00:05:31,840
It's like 50, 60 bucks a year.

65
00:05:32,240 --> 00:05:33,040
Fantastic app.

66
00:05:33,520 --> 00:05:39,040
And the fun little side too, they have videos that show you exactly how to do the exercises and stuff.

67
00:05:39,280 --> 00:05:49,520
And so she watches the videos while I do the exercises and it's a fun experience, like with my daughter, her helping me out.

68
00:05:49,680 --> 00:06:00,080
And so this app for less than ten bucks a month, like five bucks a month, is augmenting this physical in real life experience in a way that's just so incredibly valuable.

69
00:06:00,320 --> 00:06:02,400
So little three point five on your list there.

70
00:06:02,960 --> 00:06:04,720
The time you spend in the gym, right?

71
00:06:05,120 --> 00:06:11,840
You spend hours, you know, maybe two, five hours a week, you know, twenty hours a month potentially if you're serious about it.

72
00:06:12,320 --> 00:06:17,680
Think about navigation, you know, if you're a serious hobbyist, mariner, boaters, you should just call them boaters.

73
00:06:18,160 --> 00:06:20,400
I mean, that's something you're going to want to add software to, right?

74
00:06:20,960 --> 00:06:22,960
And so, yeah, this is right in my wheelhouse.

75
00:06:23,120 --> 00:06:27,520
I mean, this is why we started RevenueCAD or like why I still am excited about the project six years later.

76
00:06:27,760 --> 00:06:31,200
It's because I get to be a very tiny part of all these apps and they're still cool.

77
00:06:31,360 --> 00:06:32,640
And I still think we're early days.

78
00:06:33,040 --> 00:06:38,240
Like you were saying, Sean, there's still physical products out there that have not been disrupted by apps.

79
00:06:38,400 --> 00:06:41,680
And then maybe that's a question of time, it could be a question of technology.

80
00:06:42,240 --> 00:06:45,760
But honestly, like, I don't think we've reached even peak apps yet.

81
00:06:46,080 --> 00:06:47,280
So very exciting.

82
00:06:47,920 --> 00:06:56,960
So after HP, did you dive right into Momentum Labs and tell us a little bit about Momentum Labs before we move on to some of the other topics I wanted to cover?

83
00:06:57,280 --> 00:06:57,360
Yeah.

84
00:06:57,680 --> 00:07:04,480
I had the wonderful opportunity to work at HP for, I would argue, the best boss manager leader I've ever met.

85
00:07:05,040 --> 00:07:07,760
And so I worked with Steven at HP for a number of years.

86
00:07:08,320 --> 00:07:12,880
Post his retirement, I realized that I wasn't made for a big company.

87
00:07:13,040 --> 00:07:19,200
I had a great time working at a big company because I worked for Steven who was, as I said, truly the best leader I've ever met.

88
00:07:19,520 --> 00:07:23,840
And while working for him, I had a slightly different experience than a traditional big company experience.

89
00:07:24,320 --> 00:07:31,920
While I was at HP, one of the things that I worked with Steven on is he was brought in to take over WebOS.

90
00:07:32,320 --> 00:07:36,880
If you remember the WebOS touchpad, the HP touchpad, post the failed launch.

91
00:07:37,840 --> 00:07:44,720
And so I was intimately involved in that for about three months before HP made the decision to pull the plug on it.

92
00:07:45,200 --> 00:07:48,240
And that was really my first exposure to mobile and apps.

93
00:07:48,640 --> 00:07:55,360
And that's when I started to learn about this worldwide global distribution, the freemium model strategy.

94
00:07:55,920 --> 00:08:03,200
And Steven really used to, I can't say coined the term, but he used it all the time, where the phone is the control panel for your life.

95
00:08:03,840 --> 00:08:07,280
And you go back to this, this is 2011.

96
00:08:07,840 --> 00:08:14,560
At that time, we were really transitioning to this model where the phone is the control panel for your life and to be front and center and part of that control.

97
00:08:15,280 --> 00:08:18,560
panel was an amazing concept at that time and has proven to be right.

98
00:08:19,200 --> 00:08:24,560
Momentum Labs came from the idea of, hey, I love this idea of mobile apps.

99
00:08:24,640 --> 00:08:25,040
This is great.

100
00:08:25,200 --> 00:08:26,400
And everyone's building apps.

101
00:08:26,960 --> 00:08:28,960
And how do I get into the industry and compete?

102
00:08:29,440 --> 00:08:34,160
And I was very aware of this app store ranking mote concept.

103
00:08:34,640 --> 00:08:37,200
The top apps basically control 95% of the market.

104
00:08:37,680 --> 00:08:51,840
And as I was throwing around different app ideas with friends and a couple of people who became business partners for me, we realized that it's much harder to build something from scratch and compete when you're dealing with an incumbent that may have a million users already.

105
00:08:52,000 --> 00:09:05,920
Isn't it easier to go in and just buy someone that has already proven product market fit, scale and adoption rates, and then we can come in and apply our know-how expertise to take the business forward.

106
00:09:06,320 --> 00:09:21,520
And so that is the genesis behind Momentum Labs from day one, and I don't know if we were the first to start really buying apps, but we were definitely on the first wave of companies and firms who were buying apps in, you know, this time it was 2015.

107
00:09:21,760 --> 00:09:24,000
We started looking to acquire 2016.

108
00:09:24,240 --> 00:09:30,800
I think it was when we made our first acquisition and we wanted to come in and find opportunities to buy an app where in their category.

109
00:09:31,440 --> 00:09:38,400
where they were ranked three, four or five, and we had the set objective of moving them up two to three spots in that ranking list.

110
00:09:38,960 --> 00:09:43,440
And if we did that, our revenues didn't grow five, ten, fifteen percent.

111
00:09:44,080 --> 00:09:56,640
They grew fifty percent, eighty five percent, one hundred twenty five percent, two hundred percent because as much as the App Store has this mote, number one owns forty five percent of the market, most categories.

112
00:09:57,280 --> 00:10:01,920
Number two owns twenty five percent, number three, fifteen, number four, seven.

113
00:10:02,560 --> 00:10:06,160
So you can see how you actually double as you move up the ranking list.

114
00:10:06,720 --> 00:10:07,360
Yeah, that's great.

115
00:10:07,680 --> 00:10:13,120
Are you still buying apps or are you currently really focused on iNavacs and healthy?

116
00:10:13,600 --> 00:10:14,720
We're always looking to buy.

117
00:10:15,680 --> 00:10:18,320
So I want to say that we're always looking to buy.

118
00:10:19,120 --> 00:10:21,440
We have not bought since COVID started.

119
00:10:21,840 --> 00:10:23,200
So the end of 2019.

120
00:10:23,600 --> 00:10:25,520
And there are a few things that have happened at that time.

121
00:10:26,240 --> 00:10:30,720
Number one, if there's a good trick out there, a lot of people figure it out and start doing it.

122
00:10:31,440 --> 00:10:34,800
So we were very fortunate to start buying when we did because we're one of the first.

123
00:10:35,520 --> 00:10:41,840
I thought we were able to find to use the Warren Buffer term great businesses at fair prices.

124
00:10:42,240 --> 00:10:45,840
And we paid fair prices for our great businesses more recently.

125
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:54,080
You're paying crazy prices for fair businesses and that's a proposition that is not in line with our approach.

126
00:10:54,560 --> 00:11:04,960
So the market has got a little overheated, a little oversaturated and as a result prices have gone up and we're not seeing the quality of apps that we want.

127
00:11:05,600 --> 00:11:10,240
We have had the opportunity to sell a couple into this environment and that has been very good for us.

128
00:11:10,800 --> 00:11:19,520
And at the same time, we have two businesses that we've acquired, INEVEX and Healthy, which have really grown beyond our expectations.

129
00:11:20,160 --> 00:11:23,520
And so they have been taking up more and more of our time to help them grow.

130
00:11:23,920 --> 00:11:30,080
I will also add that we are not a firm, we're not an investing fund, we don't have LPs or investors.

131
00:11:30,640 --> 00:11:31,520
This is our own money.

132
00:11:31,760 --> 00:11:37,040
So we have the opportunity to invest our time and resources only on projects that we want to.

133
00:11:37,600 --> 00:11:40,960
I keep forgetting that you bought one of my apps back in January.

134
00:11:42,480 --> 00:11:44,400
So I don't even remember Wait, which one?

135
00:11:45,120 --> 00:11:46,320
My mileage logging app.

136
00:11:46,800 --> 00:11:47,600
Oh, yeah.

137
00:11:47,920 --> 00:11:49,840
Actually, I still, I still get a check from you.

138
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:52,480
What every quarter or something, I get a bill pay.

139
00:11:53,440 --> 00:11:59,840
It's funny because we've just moved on from that for so long that I always keep forgetting that one of the tiny parts of your portfolio.

140
00:12:00,480 --> 00:12:05,600
It's really interesting to hear an insider's perspective.tive that the market has overheated, which doesn't surprise me.

141
00:12:05,680 --> 00:12:11,200
I mean, just anecdotally from having heard more and more folks go after this model, which when there's margin, right?

142
00:12:11,440 --> 00:12:16,480
When there's an arbitrage opportunity, it gets discovered and then exploited and then used up pretty quickly.

143
00:12:16,720 --> 00:12:24,160
But yeah, I think that doesn't mean that there aren't still arbitrage opportunities within your own portfolio and like opportunities for growth.

144
00:12:24,320 --> 00:12:31,520
I think the point you made about rankings being nonlinear, I think is unintuitive to folks because you really don't see that on the App Store.

145
00:12:31,680 --> 00:12:35,360
They're presented to us in a linear way, but it's really a power law, right?

146
00:12:35,680 --> 00:12:40,240
So if you go up one step, you're going up more than a superlinear step in distribution, which.

147
00:12:40,480 --> 00:12:43,520
Which generally, I think we're evolved not to understand power law as well.

148
00:12:44,080 --> 00:12:52,640
So when you can understand that and see that, yeah, you can potentially even just to move one place, the ROI on that can be really high if you have a way to do it.

149
00:12:52,720 --> 00:12:52,880
Right.

150
00:12:53,040 --> 00:13:00,240
If you see a weak competitor or something like that, we're really interested to hear kind of how the playbook goes for you guys and when you're trying to call up the rankings.

151
00:13:00,880 --> 00:13:14,480
One of the interesting things about that power law too is that the data I app figures and sensor tower and other folks who track the top charts, they don't account very well for that.

152
00:13:14,960 --> 00:13:16,320
And we've seen that at RevenueCat.

153
00:13:16,400 --> 00:13:18,880
I mean, we've had customers hit number one in the App Store multiple times.

154
00:13:19,920 --> 00:13:24,080
We're fighting fires like keeping the servers up because the traffic is just insane.

155
00:13:24,640 --> 00:13:27,840
And then you see a report, oh, I got X downloads.

156
00:13:27,920 --> 00:13:29,280
And we're like, uh uh, no.

157
00:13:31,200 --> 00:13:32,240
That's not correct.

158
00:13:32,480 --> 00:13:40,320
So it's kind of interesting how even with the charts and with these companies tracking the charts, you just can't even take account of some of that power law distribution.

159
00:13:40,960 --> 00:13:42,000
100% correct.

160
00:13:42,160 --> 00:13:45,280
And I want to kind of add on two tangential points to this topic.

161
00:13:45,920 --> 00:13:54,000
The first one is when I say going up in the rankings, I'm talking about consistent, stable performance in the rankings.

162
00:13:54,480 --> 00:13:56,720
Now we all know we can buy rankings.

163
00:13:57,280 --> 00:14:11,440
There are companies out there that will help you buy downloads that will help you buy users that don't necessarily translate into sustainable pro or paid subscription models with strong retention and renewal rates.

164
00:14:11,680 --> 00:14:22,320
Apple and Google have learned this, in my opinion, that they understand that there are apps or businesses out there that buy rankings and those apps may show at the top of the charts on the downloads list.

165
00:14:22,800 --> 00:14:26,320
But what you want to pay attention to is not necessarily the top of the charts of the downloads list.

166
00:14:26,880 --> 00:14:30,480
It's the top of the charts of the grossings list, which is the revenues list.

167
00:14:31,040 --> 00:14:33,920
Those are the apps that are making the revenues from my perspective.

168
00:14:35,600 --> 00:14:36,000
I like money.

169
00:14:36,880 --> 00:14:37,360
Capitalist.

170
00:14:38,240 --> 00:14:46,080
I want to see the apps that impress me are those that over time can hold their position on the grossings list and move up.

171
00:14:46,240 --> 00:14:49,760
And when they do move up, you definitely see that exponential growth there.

172
00:14:50,160 --> 00:14:52,960
And how much does the search ranking factor into that?

173
00:14:53,760 --> 00:14:57,920
I think everyone has a perspective on ASO and how to game ASO.

174
00:14:58,320 --> 00:15:01,440
I don't know if there's any special source to it, a little secret.

175
00:15:02,400 --> 00:15:05,440
We generally take a very light touch to ASO.

176
00:15:05,840 --> 00:15:08,960
We do a lot of work and a lot of cleanup when we initially acquired the app.

177
00:15:09,520 --> 00:15:11,600
And then we almost like not to touch it.

178
00:15:12,320 --> 00:15:18,480
And part of our thesis has been the longevity of performance and consistency of performance.

179
00:15:18,960 --> 00:15:21,040
Our approach with Search is very much a light touch.

180
00:15:21,360 --> 00:15:33,440
But the other point I wanted to bring up, which, Jacob, I love what you were sharing earlier and it made me think back to when we started Momentum Labs, we did have some conversations with advisers and there were people who were interested in investing in this concept.

181
00:15:34,240 --> 00:15:38,160
And the question we got all the time was, what's the life cycle of an app?

182
00:15:38,640 --> 00:15:42,400
So the app has been around for a few years, three, four years, you're now looking to buy it.

183
00:15:43,040 --> 00:15:47,680
Does this thing have a three, four year additional life cycle from where we stand today?

184
00:15:48,160 --> 00:15:50,240
And I remember laughing every time I heard that question.

185
00:15:50,240 --> 00:15:57,360
That question because I knew if you bought the right app, the life cycle is twenty five, thirty years.

186
00:15:58,240 --> 00:16:05,520
You can definitely talk about games and there's a life cycle in games because of the attention span and the hype nature of the gaming category.

187
00:16:06,320 --> 00:16:12,640
But if you talk about a utility function, David, you mentioned mileage log plus, we are always going to need a mileage log tracker.

188
00:16:12,880 --> 00:16:14,400
So what's the life cycle in that app?

189
00:16:14,880 --> 00:16:23,520
twenty, thirty years easily, really until enough new competitors with better algorithms or better tech can come out to replace you.

190
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:28,640
But if you can stay on top of those tech trends, mileage log plus will be around for generations.

191
00:16:29,280 --> 00:16:30,720
Yeah, think of like Strava.

192
00:16:31,040 --> 00:16:36,800
Strava's been around since 2009, I think even earlier, and they've managed to just continue to innovate and push.

193
00:16:36,960 --> 00:16:39,040
And here they are, fifteen years later, still pushing.

194
00:16:39,520 --> 00:16:57,600
It would be interesting for someone to do a meta analysis sort of on category winner over now the decade plus of the App Store we've had and see what the churn is, because there's probably more churn in the number two, number three, but like in the number one, like which I mean for app builder folks looking to like hit it big.

195
00:16:57,840 --> 00:17:00,160
It's like, imagine what that next category is, right?

196
00:17:00,400 --> 00:17:00,960
And go and own it.

197
00:17:01,040 --> 00:17:06,720
Because even if it's small today, which people putting GPSs on their bikes was small in 2005, 2016.

198
00:17:06,720 --> 00:17:10,000
And then five, six, seven, eight, nine own it, stay on top of it.

199
00:17:10,320 --> 00:17:15,280
And that's going to be, like you said, a generational company for something that seems very much like a utility, right?

200
00:17:15,680 --> 00:17:21,280
But that will be a revenue generating and a growth source of not just cash flow.

201
00:17:21,600 --> 00:17:38,720
I also believe these are growth equities still, maybe they're not in their fifteenth year, then maybe it's not like in their first five years or something like this, but I still think it's going to outcompete a lot of different places you could put money most likely, just because of also all the still underlying trends, the devices are still improving, the technology is accelerating and mobile.

202
00:17:39,040 --> 00:17:55,360
I mean, we're seeing this now with GPTs and AI, like mobile is going to benefit from that technology mobile as a delivery platform now for basically any you know, now that connectivity is so good and that like it basically doesn't matter if you do it on the device or in the cloud, like cost wise, it's not that big of a difference.

203
00:17:55,840 --> 00:18:05,200
You'll be able to port all of that and you can expand that to VR and AR, like those are trends that I don't think really have hit commercial viability yet and those likely will in the next decade.

204
00:18:05,840 --> 00:18:11,440
So yeah, I think you should start stashing some capital because you might want to buy up some new ones in the next ten years.

205
00:18:13,280 --> 00:18:18,320
Anyway, I'll stop waxing philosophical about technology and apps because, you know, I'm kind of into them.

206
00:18:18,880 --> 00:18:23,680
So one of the main things I wanted to get your insights on is freemium strategy.

207
00:18:23,840 --> 00:18:26,000
I know this is something you think a ton about.

208
00:18:26,560 --> 00:18:32,080
So first let's just talk about why use freemium and why you think it's so important to an app.

209
00:18:32,720 --> 00:18:37,360
I have probably seventeen points I want to share here, but I'm going to start at the top.

210
00:18:37,600 --> 00:18:42,240
And I think it's important to talk about freemium to start with the concept of subscription.

211
00:18:42,640 --> 00:18:46,320
When the app store was launched, remember there were paid apps and there were free apps.

212
00:18:46,960 --> 00:18:49,200
And then there were in-app purchases consumables.

213
00:18:49,920 --> 00:18:56,160
But the industry really changed or evolved or found its footing when subscriptions came about.

214
00:18:56,640 --> 00:19:01,120
And there was initial backlash from consumers against subscriptions..

215
00:19:01,440 --> 00:19:06,560
You know, if I pay you today, why do you now need me to pay you every week or every month or every year?

216
00:19:06,880 --> 00:19:10,800
But we as developers know that software and app is a living thing.

217
00:19:11,680 --> 00:19:12,560
It takes work.

218
00:19:13,280 --> 00:19:15,040
You have to invest in it every single day.

219
00:19:15,200 --> 00:19:17,440
You can't just build one and have it live forever.

220
00:19:17,920 --> 00:19:22,480
The subscription model, I think, aligned the developer and the customer or the user.

221
00:19:22,960 --> 00:19:25,040
And I think it's fundamental to kind of understand that.

222
00:19:25,840 --> 00:19:29,200
The next point is the strategy behind your product.

223
00:19:29,840 --> 00:19:31,040
Why do freemiums work?

224
00:19:31,360 --> 00:19:33,680
It gives somebody the opportunity to try before they buy.

225
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:38,960
So the first concept around freemium is, can you give your customers enough to be able to buy?

226
00:19:38,960 --> 00:19:47,920
Is enough of your product for them to try it, see if they like it, and then is there a hook or an incentive or an additional feature that will get them to start paying for it?

227
00:19:48,560 --> 00:20:02,320
The strategy around where that point is, I think is 90% of the success of an app or of the app's business model, but in my opinion, I think developers spend 10% of their time thinking about it.

228
00:20:02,880 --> 00:20:09,440
If you can get that right, and it's not to say you get it right right right right, because you have an initial idea or a strategy, test it.

229
00:20:09,920 --> 00:20:10,720
AB test it.

230
00:20:11,280 --> 00:20:13,280
Put this feature on the side of the paywall.

231
00:20:13,520 --> 00:20:14,400
Now move it free.

232
00:20:14,720 --> 00:20:15,360
See what happens.

233
00:20:15,680 --> 00:20:22,320
And when I say strategy., it's not just kind of features, it's also how you want to engage with your user base.

234
00:20:22,960 --> 00:20:28,880
So I'll give you an example and David, I know you and I have talked about this and I think it's great to share it here, which is Healthy.

235
00:20:29,520 --> 00:20:30,880
Healthy is an app that we have.

236
00:20:31,440 --> 00:20:34,160
It's one of the largest food tracking and diet apps.

237
00:20:34,480 --> 00:20:41,600
There's a very core function around diets or fitness, which is while it is something that we do individually.

238
00:20:41,920 --> 00:20:46,800
David, you mentioned working out in your garage with an app, you do that yourself.

239
00:20:47,040 --> 00:20:54,480
People are on a weight loss journey, are making decisions themselves every day about what they eat, how they meal prep and the emotional journey that they go through.

240
00:20:54,960 --> 00:20:56,960
The commitment to it is something they do by themselves.

241
00:20:57,520 --> 00:21:00,880
However, you mentioned incorporating your daughter as your personal trainer.

242
00:21:01,120 --> 00:21:10,160
The concept around a health and wellness journey is actually better done with others because we need support, we need motivation, accountability, sometimes advice.

243
00:21:10,640 --> 00:21:13,760
And so within Healthy, we built a community feature.

244
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:15,040
It's a function in the app.

245
00:21:15,200 --> 00:21:16,640
It serves as essentially a mini Facebook.

246
00:21:17,360 --> 00:21:21,440
What we did is we made viewing the community free.

247
00:21:21,840 --> 00:21:24,000
If you're a free user, you get to see the community.

248
00:21:24,400 --> 00:21:29,360
You get to see the comments, you get to see the threads, you get to see the advice that's shared, you get to engage.

249
00:21:30,160 --> 00:21:35,040
However, we made paying a component of for writing into the community.

250
00:21:35,520 --> 00:21:40,000
You can only post if you are a paying member of our pro service.

251
00:21:40,640 --> 00:21:45,520
And that kind of puts a really big line in the sand in a very good way for us.

252
00:21:45,840 --> 00:21:51,760
First and foremost, when we talk about weight and weight loss, it is a very sensitive topic for many people.

253
00:21:52,160 --> 00:21:56,080
And unfortunately, fat shaming and bullying is real in our society.

254
00:21:56,640 --> 00:22:00,240
What we learned is bullies and trolls won't pay.

255
00:22:00,720 --> 00:22:06,000
So by making posting part of a pro service, we kept our community clean overnight.

256
00:22:06,720 --> 00:22:08,240
And our community values that.

257
00:22:08,720 --> 00:22:09,840
And they rally around it.

258
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:10,800
And they support each other.

259
00:22:10,800 --> 00:22:18,480
And today, if you go out into our industry and you talk to people about healthy, one of the first things they'll tell you is we love the community.

260
00:22:18,720 --> 00:22:23,920
And it's because we prized its wholesomeness and the collaborative nature that comes from it.

261
00:22:24,400 --> 00:22:28,640
And that came from making a decision about where the paywall sat with the community.

262
00:22:29,360 --> 00:22:34,240
But remember, if the community is so important to us, we can't have it be behind closed doors.

263
00:22:34,480 --> 00:22:36,320
We have to show it and share it with everyone.

264
00:22:36,720 --> 00:22:38,160
And we have to pull people into it.

265
00:22:38,640 --> 00:22:40,320
And that's why viewing is free.

266
00:22:40,800 --> 00:22:45,040
You made such a good point around testing the freemium strategy.

267
00:22:45,360 --> 00:22:48,720
There's so much talk about paywall testing, about onboard testing.

268
00:22:49,120 --> 00:22:58,640
about like test, test, test, test, test, but very little talk about testing the actual premium strategy of what's behind the paywall, what's not.

269
00:22:59,120 --> 00:23:02,560
I mean, arguably, way more leverage than what's on the paywall, right?

270
00:23:02,720 --> 00:23:06,400
I mean, what's on the paywall is downstream of what's actually behind the paywall, right?

271
00:23:06,640 --> 00:23:10,400
Like, what it looks like is, you know, it goes back to value.

272
00:23:10,640 --> 00:23:11,760
It's again like money talk.

273
00:23:12,080 --> 00:23:15,120
So figuring out where people are going to pay, like that's your best testing strategy.

274
00:23:15,280 --> 00:23:16,240
Sorry, back to you, Dave.

275
00:23:16,480 --> 00:23:18,960
Yeah, well, and then free trial versus no free trial.

276
00:23:19,040 --> 00:23:20,640
And actually, I'd love for you to talk about that.

277
00:23:20,720 --> 00:23:23,680
So if I remember correctly, you don't have a free trial in Healthy.

278
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:26,560
I have never done free trial in any of my apps.

279
00:23:27,040 --> 00:23:32,400
There are many apps and companies I've mentored, helped, consulted with that have tried it.

280
00:23:32,800 --> 00:23:33,920
I've never seen it work.

281
00:23:34,800 --> 00:23:46,880
One concept or assumption that I've had, it's a thesis probably, is I don't like the idea of taking something away from someone in a free trial, you're taking it away from them.

282
00:23:47,280 --> 00:23:52,240
And I don't like that because it creates almost a negative experience.

283
00:23:52,640 --> 00:23:55,600
I get the notion of here, try it, see how great it is.

284
00:23:55,840 --> 00:23:58,000
Now you're going to lose it and you're going to want it back.

285
00:23:58,720 --> 00:24:00,480
It just doesn't feel right to me.

286
00:24:01,040 --> 00:24:10,320
I would rather have every moment be a delightful experience, then put a user in a position where they can have a negative engagement with my product or brand.

287
00:24:10,720 --> 00:24:13,760
I've also found that the conversion rates don't pay out.

288
00:24:14,320 --> 00:24:15,760
It becomes a very hard process.

289
00:24:17,040 --> 00:24:22,480
One thing that people try to do with the free trial concept is it's free, but give me your credit card today.

290
00:24:22,800 --> 00:24:25,520
Don't worry, I'm not going to charge you for seven days or 30 days.

291
00:24:26,320 --> 00:24:27,440
Users don't really like that.

292
00:24:27,840 --> 00:24:34,880
Because even if I'm taking out my credit card and not paying, subliminally, I am taking out my credit card and paying, even if you tell me you're not going to charge me.

293
00:24:35,280 --> 00:24:50,960
So there's just too many maybe trouble points on that engagement flow that I think cause friction and I've never found the model to actually outperform a very simple free to freemium upgrade.

294
00:24:51,440 --> 00:24:54,560
And Jacob, you're talking about these delightful upgrade screens.

295
00:24:55,120 --> 00:25:04,640
You can have the best conversion flow, the best upgrade screens, but if you haven't hooked your user on the freemium product yet, does anybody even matter?

296
00:25:04,880 --> 00:25:13,760
I mean, and this is, you know, sort of plug revenue cat, some of what I hope our testing tools can provide is like a model of revenue model for an app like this has a bunch of inputs.

297
00:25:14,000 --> 00:25:19,600
One is how many people download, if there's a trial, how many people start a trial, and then how many people convert, and then how long they retain.

298
00:25:20,080 --> 00:25:26,880
The difficult aspect of testing these things is that all of those variables are correlated, or at least those populations are related.

299
00:25:27,120 --> 00:25:36,320
So you can add a trial and you might convert more trials or start more trials, but you're definitely going to lower your conversion to paid rate and you're definitely going to lower your retention rate.

300
00:25:36,480 --> 00:25:57,680
And then vice versa, you can cut the trial, force everyone to pay real money to get any access, and you're definitely going to reduce your total probably conversion to paid, but I would venture to guess you're going to increase your retention because everyone who does pay will have a higher affinity and just be more engaged in the product than someone who glibly starts a trial, forgets about it.

301
00:25:58,240 --> 00:26:02,400
That is a percentage of trial conversions of people, you know, and that's why you ask for credit.

302
00:26:02,560 --> 00:26:14,240
I mean, we can act all innocent about it, but we all know that's part of the game is like getting people on a trial and then kind of hoping they forget and stay with us, which I agree with you from a consumer product builder perspective doesn't feel very good.

303
00:26:14,800 --> 00:26:18,000
But I will push back and say, like, I know a lot of apps that make it work.

304
00:26:18,320 --> 00:26:20,560
I would wonder though, if they've tested it fully, right?

305
00:26:20,720 --> 00:26:25,200
Like, I would wonder though, if they've fully gone, hey, let's forget about this trial.

306
00:26:25,600 --> 00:26:33,360
Let's try to set up a free experience that you can experience without a trial that's attractive and engaging and then fully get folks to upgrade.

307
00:26:33,600 --> 00:26:36,640
I would be really curious if folks push that and actually do that well.

308
00:26:37,120 --> 00:26:37,840
instead of focusing.

309
00:26:38,480 --> 00:26:42,080
I tend to believe if your app delivers X value, you're going to extract.

310
00:26:42,960 --> 00:26:46,320
Y X or whatever dollars from it, right?

311
00:26:46,560 --> 00:26:51,040
You can move that up and down a little bit, but the bigger variable is going to be that X in value delivery.

312
00:26:51,520 --> 00:26:55,360
And so whether you have a trial or not, it's all kind of shake out and you'll kind of optimize.

313
00:26:55,760 --> 00:27:00,400
These are hard tests to run because it's hard to take an entire app and be like, I'm going to throw out the trial and re run.

314
00:27:00,480 --> 00:27:01,920
So I understand why people don't do it.

315
00:27:02,320 --> 00:27:06,400
And I honestly at first I was like, oh, Sean's got a thing against free trials.

316
00:27:06,560 --> 00:27:09,920
But now that I'm thinking through it, I'm like, I think it's a pretty reasonable stance.

317
00:27:10,160 --> 00:27:13,360
And actually, that's why I was like, I would love to see some really good data.

318
00:27:13,600 --> 00:27:17,920
Like we could really well AB test it it because I think I would almost stand with you there.

319
00:27:18,400 --> 00:27:20,400
My assumptions would be that maybe that would work better.

320
00:27:20,800 --> 00:27:26,400
I think also thinking about healthy as an example, I think there's real commitment psychology about it too, right?

321
00:27:26,640 --> 00:27:27,760
You get people to pay in advance.

322
00:27:27,920 --> 00:27:29,040
They're like starting a journey.

323
00:27:29,280 --> 00:27:30,320
They want to like put that down.

324
00:27:30,800 --> 00:27:38,720
If you do a trial, there's a good chance you'll lose them in that trial period because they haven't really committed anything yet, which for that particular app could be a specific case.

325
00:27:39,360 --> 00:27:45,120
But I think in general, yeah, I was even thinking about some utility I was using to do CAD recently and it had a free trial.

326
00:27:45,440 --> 00:27:47,040
I was like, I need to design one part.

327
00:27:47,680 --> 00:27:49,840
I'm just going to do the trial and cancel, right?

328
00:27:50,160 --> 00:27:52,720
Like they extracted nothing from me, which is unfortunate.

329
00:27:53,120 --> 00:27:54,960
because I definitely extracted value from the product.

330
00:27:55,200 --> 00:28:02,880
Like I would have preferred to pay them something, but because I'm also a rational economic actor, I was like, not going to let that happen.

331
00:28:03,120 --> 00:28:06,000
So yeah, anyway, I think that's an interesting and somewhat controversial take.

332
00:28:06,560 --> 00:28:24,720
The key to not having a free trial is the freemium strategy, so tell me your thinking about how you decided what's free because the free product has to deliver some value to keep people engaged, but then you need some other features that deliver more value that people are actually willing to pay for.

333
00:28:25,200 --> 00:28:27,360
And getting that right really is the crux.

334
00:28:27,680 --> 00:28:29,360
So how do you think about that?

335
00:28:30,000 --> 00:28:30,560
We were both talking.

336
00:28:31,120 --> 00:28:35,520
I was thinking of a great example to have a discussion with Kia right now about.

337
00:28:35,840 --> 00:28:37,360
David, let's take you back to your garage.

338
00:28:37,520 --> 00:28:38,880
You're working out on a fitness app.

339
00:28:39,280 --> 00:28:41,120
So let's talk through two scenarios.

340
00:28:42,080 --> 00:28:44,640
Scenario A is a free trial.

341
00:28:45,040 --> 00:28:52,000
So you download an app, you go to put in your credit card and you get either a seven or fourteen day free trial and you can start working out.

342
00:28:52,560 --> 00:28:56,400
When you have to put in that credit card to start the free trial, you have a moment's pause right there.

343
00:28:56,800 --> 00:28:56,960
Right?

344
00:28:57,120 --> 00:28:57,840
We just do.

345
00:28:58,320 --> 00:28:59,760
Not everybody's going to put in their credit card.

346
00:29:00,080 --> 00:29:08,800
Jake Moore at Superwall shared some data with me that the number of people who tap a purchase button and then dismiss the actual payment.

347
00:29:08,800 --> 00:29:10,880
And then, of course, the actual payment screen is massive.

348
00:29:11,200 --> 00:29:19,600
In his tracking, it was something like sixty percent of people will tap the continue or the start free trial or whatever button, but then not actually pay.

349
00:29:19,760 --> 00:29:20,960
So yeah, you're exactly right.

350
00:29:21,280 --> 00:29:22,880
It's a huge moment of pause.

351
00:29:23,360 --> 00:29:23,600
It is.

352
00:29:24,080 --> 00:29:25,760
But now let's keep talking through this.

353
00:29:25,840 --> 00:29:34,720
So you do put your credit card details in, you start your seven or fourteen day free trial, you start working out, and at the end of the seven or fourteen days, you have another decision to make.

354
00:29:35,440 --> 00:29:39,360
So now I'm asking you to actually stop and think about your purchase twice.

355
00:29:40,000 --> 00:29:46,000
And both times are probably not a good pleasurable experience because you're leaning towards the negative first.

356
00:29:46,640 --> 00:29:52,640
So that's scenario A and maybe you continue using the app so you keep paying for it and you're great.

357
00:29:53,280 --> 00:30:00,880
Scenario B, it's the exact same app, it's the exact same service, but I'm going to put you on a free trial where you get two workouts for free a week.

358
00:30:01,440 --> 00:30:10,080
You can use the app indefinitely if you just take two workouts a week and if you want more than two workouts a week, you're going to upgrade to our subscription.

359
00:30:10,400 --> 00:30:11,200
So what happens here?

360
00:30:12,000 --> 00:30:16,400
I get you into the app with no moments of pause or no boundaries to the experience.

361
00:30:17,040 --> 00:30:18,160
I get you using the product.

362
00:30:18,720 --> 00:30:24,880
If you like the product, you can keep using it for free with a very good understanding of where the limitations are.

363
00:30:24,880 --> 00:30:26,080
So now you have a better understanding of where the limitation is.

364
00:30:26,560 --> 00:30:36,000
If you like the product more or want to work out more, I'm now asking you to make a purchase from a perspective or a position where you have a positive engagement with me.

365
00:30:36,400 --> 00:30:39,680
So now when you hit that upgrade screen, what do you think the conversion rate is?

366
00:30:39,920 --> 00:30:41,360
What do you think the drop off rate is?

367
00:30:41,600 --> 00:30:48,160
Now I'm not saying every single app is the same because different business models, different products, different industries, different customers.

368
00:30:48,960 --> 00:30:57,920
But in this instance, and you want to do the AB test, Jacob, I would love to know what the end performance is on scenario A and scenario B.

369
00:30:58,160 --> 00:30:59,680
And I'd love to know your perspectives too.

370
00:31:02,800 --> 00:31:10,960
What I actually really appreciated was that they gave you three free workouts and every feature was unlocked.

371
00:31:11,200 --> 00:31:12,560
You got the full experience.

372
00:31:12,800 --> 00:31:17,120
So it wasn't exactly what you were saying because I did end up in that moment of decision.

373
00:31:17,840 --> 00:31:25,200
But what I thought was fantastic about their freemium strategy is that I got the whole app and I freaking loved it.

374
00:31:25,520 --> 00:31:26,960
Like I was so impressed.

375
00:31:27,520 --> 00:31:28,800
I didn't have to start a trial.

376
00:31:29,040 --> 00:31:32,880
You just open the app and start working out and you get three workouts.

377
00:31:33,200 --> 00:31:35,920
It didn't make it super clear the very first time I was like, what's going on?

378
00:31:36,000 --> 00:31:40,000
But as soon as I finished my first workout, it said, you have two more workouts for free.

379
00:31:40,480 --> 00:31:54,800
It set the expectations, but one of the challenges of a good freemium experience is if you're putting so many features behind the paywall that are actually core experiences of the app, then you're not creating a great experience.

380
00:31:55,200 --> 00:31:58,240
Well, I mean, at that point you have to have the paid trial unlock everything.

381
00:31:58,480 --> 00:31:59,440
You have to have something, right?

382
00:31:59,760 --> 00:32:03,280
The healthy example of commenting that's pretty clear and you can continue.

383
00:32:03,760 --> 00:32:06,320
I think that's the challenge with the freemium I've always had.

384
00:32:06,480 --> 00:32:13,360
I mean, I can go back to my experience at Elevate while I was there working on where we put the paywall and how much we let people have.

385
00:32:13,600 --> 00:32:18,160
And we eventually settled on, hey, like you have to start a trial or the experience is going to be pretty bad.

386
00:32:19,040 --> 00:32:26,000
And the upsides and downsides of that, like we get a big rocket in trials and all this stuff, but I think just in general, like less affinity.

387
00:32:26,400 --> 00:32:35,280
And I think something else worth weighing here is not just the upsides and downsides of having a large nonpaying user base.

388
00:32:35,520 --> 00:32:44,240
So there's upsides in that every user who uses your app and is happy, whether they pay or not, is a potential conduit for growth.

389
00:32:44,480 --> 00:32:50,560
They will tell their friends, they will write reviews, some percentage of them will help you be brand advocates and there's ways you can improve that and all that stuff.

390
00:32:50,720 --> 00:33:05,200
And that's like truly, you know, we talk about margins, like that's a source of low cost growth that you really have to exploit in order to maintain good margins and kicking out most of your users that are somewhat interested and you could make happy is like antithetical.

391
00:33:05,440 --> 00:33:07,920
I mean, it's part of the reason revenue cap prices the way we do.

392
00:33:08,000 --> 00:33:09,520
Like, no offense to all my indies.

393
00:33:09,680 --> 00:33:14,160
I love my indies, but you all don't make me a ton of money, but like, that's on purpose, right?

394
00:33:14,400 --> 00:33:17,520
Like, because I want to serve you and if you tell me the product's bad, it's probably bad.

395
00:33:17,920 --> 00:33:19,120
And like, you're going to tell your friends.

396
00:33:19,280 --> 00:33:20,000
That's why we do it.

397
00:33:20,160 --> 00:33:22,400
And there's a similar argument for consumer stuff.

398
00:33:22,640 --> 00:33:25,120
On the flip side of that, there's also the downsides and the cost.

399
00:33:25,440 --> 00:33:27,120
So you have to support those folks.

400
00:33:27,200 --> 00:33:35,840
Like, you get something out of them, but I don't know what kind of splits, but if I had to guess, it's probably sub ten percent of your users are premium at any given time.

401
00:33:36,080 --> 00:33:44,880
So it's like 90% of your support, 90% of your server cost, 90% of your everything comes from nonpaying users, which can be counterintuitive, right?

402
00:33:45,120 --> 00:33:53,840
Or like potentially makes you question the investment, which I think was, you know, for us, again, this is seven or eight years ago, deciding at Elevate how to do it, that came into the calculation.

403
00:33:54,000 --> 00:33:56,080
It was like, hey, we get a ton of tickets and stuff like this.

404
00:33:56,320 --> 00:34:00,640
People should pay us if we're going to be doing this, which again, got away with the upsides.

405
00:34:00,800 --> 00:34:04,000
And again, unfortunately, it's not really possible yet.

406
00:34:04,240 --> 00:34:10,000
Maybe GPT solves this to build an entire world model for apps that can take all that into account.

407
00:34:10,320 --> 00:34:12,560
But I'm curious, Sean, like, how do you guys, given that.

408
00:34:12,640 --> 00:34:20,640
Given that you have this big freemium support user base, like how do you think about supporting them through customer support and back end support and like all that stuff?

409
00:34:20,800 --> 00:34:22,000
How do you break that down in your mind?

410
00:34:22,400 --> 00:34:23,920
So there are two things I wanted to bring up here.

411
00:34:24,080 --> 00:34:25,440
JKBV, just bear with me.

412
00:34:25,520 --> 00:34:28,000
I'm going to come to your question in a second, but I have a whole day.

413
00:34:29,440 --> 00:34:33,040
I thought of when we were talking about the freemium model, where it sits, what is free.

414
00:34:33,600 --> 00:34:38,720
The best freemium model I think I've ever seen really was invented by the gaming industry.

415
00:34:39,040 --> 00:34:46,000
I think of an app like Clash of Clans where you have to build or mine or attack and there's a time delay function in the app.

416
00:34:46,640 --> 00:34:49,680
So you have to mine for gems or oil or whatever it is in different games.

417
00:34:50,240 --> 00:34:52,240
But that mining can take ten minutes.

418
00:34:52,640 --> 00:34:55,280
But hey, give us 99 cents and it'll be done in ten seconds.

419
00:34:55,920 --> 00:35:00,080
That concept, that freemium strategy, that is brilliant.

420
00:35:00,800 --> 00:35:03,040
So I think that is kind of the best use case I've seen.

421
00:35:03,360 --> 00:35:09,520
For us, Jacob, how do we think about essentially absorbing the cost of supporting a freemium or a free community?

422
00:35:09,840 --> 00:35:11,440
It really comes down to the word community.

423
00:35:11,920 --> 00:35:20,880
When we talk about apps, when we talk about life cycle of apps, when we talk about moats, the biggest, best thing that you can have, the best asset for your business is a community.

424
00:35:21,360 --> 00:35:28,880
If you have people that can rally around your product, that can support each other, that can really be an engaged community, that is irreplaceable..

425
00:35:29,200 --> 00:35:33,120
And as we know when we talk about community and marketplaces, the bigger the better.

426
00:35:33,760 --> 00:35:45,120
I'd rather have a community of a million people than a community of 15,000, even if the most of the people in that million cohort are free because they are actually adding value to everyone.

427
00:35:45,440 --> 00:35:46,960
They're keeping everyone plugged in.

428
00:35:47,520 --> 00:35:59,200
And if you have a good business, there should be some type of dynamic which says, the longer someone stays involved and engaged with your product, the more likely they are to buy.

429
00:35:59,680 --> 00:36:02,560
Yeah, the long, long, long term conversion window, right?

430
00:36:02,880 --> 00:36:03,280
Like years.

431
00:36:03,840 --> 00:36:04,160
Correct.

432
00:36:04,400 --> 00:36:09,440
So if you can build the engagement into it., you should see conversion rates go up over time.

433
00:36:10,160 --> 00:36:19,200
The other thing that we do within Healthy, and it is important to kind of understand this, Healthy, which started off as an app, is now a full diet and weight loss business.

434
00:36:19,920 --> 00:36:21,680
So we have paid coaching.

435
00:36:22,320 --> 00:36:26,800
We have an online e commerce store where we sell branded products that help in a weight loss journey.

436
00:36:27,200 --> 00:36:37,600
So we find other ways to provide value to our members, and we hope that that value parlays into them essentially opening up their personal wallet and buying more products and services from us.

437
00:36:38,320 --> 00:36:41,840
And Healthy is unique because, David, I keep coming back to you in the garage.

438
00:36:42,160 --> 00:36:45,680
If you are starting a wellness journey, you don't just have download an app.

439
00:36:46,160 --> 00:36:51,760
I'm going to take a bet that David, you visited Lululemon either before or after downloading the app.

440
00:36:52,160 --> 00:36:55,280
Maybe you bought a new pair of shoes from Nike or Adidas or on.

441
00:36:55,920 --> 00:36:57,760
There is a bigger investment.

442
00:36:58,000 --> 00:37:02,960
And when I say investment, it's not always monetary, but an emotional investment in committing to that journey.

443
00:37:03,440 --> 00:37:12,880
And so we have an opportunity with Healthy to be a partner with someone in that journey and be able to help them, not just in an app, but in the community and with coaching with products.

444
00:37:13,520 --> 00:37:17,680
Have you modeled out what that journey looks like for your freemium base?

445
00:37:18,400 --> 00:37:21,680
How long it takes for a person to eventually subscribe?

446
00:37:21,920 --> 00:37:25,760
What percentage of your free user base does eventually subscribe?

447
00:37:26,080 --> 00:37:27,440
What does that look like for Healthy?

448
00:37:28,000 --> 00:37:29,120
Our numbers are strong.

449
00:37:29,520 --> 00:37:34,720
And one of the reasons our numbers are strong is because we started off as an app business first.

450
00:37:35,360 --> 00:37:40,960
We are the low cost provider in the industry, but we can still deliver exceptional value and quality.

451
00:37:41,360 --> 00:37:44,480
So, you know this notion, better, faster, cheaper, you can only have two.

452
00:37:44,720 --> 00:37:45,680
Don't ever ask for three.

453
00:37:46,160 --> 00:37:47,440
We give our users all three.

454
00:37:48,000 --> 00:37:50,720
So the pro service for Healthy is thirty dollars a year.

455
00:37:51,200 --> 00:37:55,840
Our two biggest competitors, Weight Watchers and Noom, neither have a free product.

456
00:37:56,160 --> 00:37:56,880
They're paid only.

457
00:37:57,600 --> 00:38:02,640
Weight Watchers will run you more than two hundred and thirty dollars a year., that's their starting price.

458
00:38:02,960 --> 00:38:04,960
And Noom's starting price is over 400.

459
00:38:05,360 --> 00:38:08,160
And so when you look at that, they don't offer a free product.

460
00:38:08,640 --> 00:38:08,880
We do.

461
00:38:09,280 --> 00:38:13,520
And our paid service, which is the same as theirs, is 30 dollars versus 100 dollars.

462
00:38:14,240 --> 00:38:20,320
So part of our sweet spot of success has been how we position ourselves against our competitors.

463
00:38:21,040 --> 00:38:23,600
When you leverage community too, it's the right strategy, right?

464
00:38:23,840 --> 00:38:29,440
Like you can lower your price point and then you have to hit them on the community aspect because they can't do it at 400 dollars ahead.

465
00:38:29,680 --> 00:38:32,640
Maybe they can, but not at the same scale that you're going to be able to do it.

466
00:38:33,040 --> 00:38:36,160
It's a good lesson too for how you go against big brands like that.

467
00:38:36,400 --> 00:38:37,360
Noom is massive.

468
00:38:37,520 --> 00:38:40,960
I don't know how big they are, but you hear them on the radio, that's big in my mind.

469
00:38:41,120 --> 00:38:44,000
And Weight Watchers has been a brand as long as I can remember.

470
00:38:44,480 --> 00:38:47,200
But I think that's also the kind of exciting thing about apps, right?

471
00:38:47,440 --> 00:38:53,040
Like we can jump in the arena with these folks and probably beat them if we play our cards right.

472
00:38:53,280 --> 00:38:56,080
Like this is probably true outside of the space as well.

473
00:38:56,240 --> 00:38:57,200
It's like nimble.

474
00:38:58,080 --> 00:39:02,880
And that's why you see category winners, I think in so many categories, are not long term.

475
00:39:03,280 --> 00:39:05,840
Like the top cycling app is Strava.

476
00:39:06,080 --> 00:39:06,800
It's not Garmin.

477
00:39:07,120 --> 00:39:08,000
No offense to Garmin.

478
00:39:08,400 --> 00:39:10,640
But like this is still an industry for new apps.

479
00:39:10,720 --> 00:39:12,400
So it's really cool to hear how you think about it.

480
00:39:12,560 --> 00:39:14,480
And like neither of those are necessarily right.

481
00:39:14,800 --> 00:39:16,320
Noom is deploying one strategy.

482
00:39:16,880 --> 00:39:21,040
Weight Watchers is another strategy and you all with the community focus have found another.

483
00:39:21,600 --> 00:39:36,080
I think there's a tendency and I think I'm as guilty about this as anyone else is to reduce apps to a model and be like, what I was talking about before, downloads to conversions to trials to whatever, you multiply it through and then you figure out your CAC and then boom, that's how big your business can be.

484
00:39:36,560 --> 00:39:41,280
And as I learn and meet more businesses, I'm learning that, well, that's not always really true.

485
00:39:41,760 --> 00:39:52,000
Maybe it's in the long messy middle of apps, but like category winners that we talk to on this podcast always have some magic that's not part in that formula, right?

486
00:39:52,240 --> 00:39:55,440
And I think you's true.

487
00:39:55,520 --> 00:40:04,000
And I think it's good advice for founders or app developers thinking about if they're entering an already crowded category or even just like defining something new is like, what's your magic going to be?

488
00:40:04,160 --> 00:40:05,280
You have to figure something out.

489
00:40:05,600 --> 00:40:06,400
Maybe it's technology.

490
00:40:06,560 --> 00:40:07,360
Maybe it's just product.

491
00:40:08,000 --> 00:40:10,240
But often it has to be a couple of things, right?

492
00:40:10,480 --> 00:40:13,600
Like you said, fast, cheap and whatever, like you give them all three, right?

493
00:40:13,840 --> 00:40:15,200
Find out a way to give people all three.

494
00:40:15,920 --> 00:40:20,240
One of the most leveraged things in subscription apps is retention.

495
00:40:20,800 --> 00:40:26,160
And we don't talk enough about price in the context of retention.

496
00:40:26,480 --> 00:40:32,560
And I would imagine that at a $30 price point, your retention is leagues ahead of new.

497
00:40:32,880 --> 00:40:36,080
Noom, Weight Watchers and others in the industry.

498
00:40:36,560 --> 00:40:48,000
So while your initial one year LTV might be multiples lower, you might start to catch up with that over the years when people look at it and are like, I'm still using the app a little.

499
00:40:48,240 --> 00:40:49,360
Thirty bucks is pretty cheap.

500
00:40:49,440 --> 00:40:50,960
Like, it's not that big a deal.

501
00:40:51,760 --> 00:40:55,920
I do think prices is an interesting leverage point for retention as well.

502
00:40:56,640 --> 00:40:56,880
Agreed.

503
00:40:57,040 --> 00:40:59,040
I think everything comes together.

504
00:40:59,760 --> 00:41:09,840
If anything, I would say a lot of people in our industry get maybe a little too singular focused on things like product market fit, conversion rates, engagement rates, retention rates.

505
00:41:10,400 --> 00:41:13,120
Which all come back to this product market fit equation.

506
00:41:13,280 --> 00:41:17,440
The mental model exercise I would challenge everyone to go through is strategy.

507
00:41:18,000 --> 00:41:25,840
Thinking through where your product fits into somebody's life, their life cycle, their emotional journey and the decisions that they make day to day.

508
00:41:26,480 --> 00:41:35,120
Because there is some combination that says, you can have the best app, but if you don't touch the right person at the right time in their life, it's not going to hit.

509
00:41:35,760 --> 00:41:40,640
So understanding where they are emotionally and with their life journey, that will work.

510
00:41:41,200 --> 00:41:48,720
So putting the two together, both the tactical of product market fit and the strategic to understand where your product fits.

511
00:41:49,520 --> 00:41:56,400
into their life and how and when you get those two rights, I think everyone's going to find a great devil of success.

512
00:41:57,040 --> 00:41:59,680
Well, I think that's a fantastic place to wrap up.

513
00:41:59,840 --> 00:42:07,920
I wish we could talk for two more hours because we only got to topic one of four that I wanted to talk to you about, but that's just so key.

514
00:42:08,160 --> 00:42:12,960
All app developers need to think more about how the app really fits into somebody's life.

515
00:42:13,200 --> 00:42:19,280
And for a subscription app for them to keep paying you year over year, month over month, it has to find that place in their life.

